  "narrative": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">This 2024 eLife article presents a brainstem-circuit account of cough-like airway defensive behaviors in mice, using an experimental neuroscience approach centered on respiratory physiology and neural stimulation. The record shows early-stage scholarly visibility: the article is open access, has zero OpenAlex citations so far, and currently has no scite signals or event-data mentions. Available metadata also indicate repository availability and dataset citation handling, suggesting the work was made accessible and linkable for reuse even though uptake has not yet accumulated in the public record.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">A brainstem circuit controls cough-like airway defensive behaviors in mice is a neuroscience article published in eLife in 2024 by a multi-institution team spanning ShanghaiTech University, Guangzhou Medical University, the University of Science and Technology of China, and Huazhong University of Science and Technology. The topic sits at the intersection of brainstem circuitry, reflex control, airway biology, and optogenetics, reflecting a mechanistic study of how central neural pathways shape defensive respiratory responses.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">From the abstract and concept profile, the work addresses a specific gap in airway-control biology: while vagal sensory neurons are increasingly recognized as heterogeneous mediators of respiratory function, the central circuits that govern airway defensive behaviors remain less well understood. The article\u2019s experimental framing, including whole-body plethysmography alongside audio and video tracking, signals a careful attempt to connect neural manipulation with observable breathing- and cough-like outputs in mice.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">In terms of measurable impact, the evidence base is still limited and should be interpreted cautiously. The work has no citations in OpenAlex at present and no scite citation record or event-data mentions in the digest. That does not diminish the study\u2019s potential relevance; it does indicate that the publication is still early in its citation life cycle, with attention not yet visible in the sources captured here.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">What is visible is accessibility and reuse infrastructure. The article is marked gold open access, appears in OpenAIRE as openly available in multiple instances, and Crossref data-citation processing succeeded for both datasets cited and cited-as-dataset signals. Together, these signs suggest a paper positioned for redistribution and methodological follow-on, even though the current record does not yet show downstream scholarly uptake.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Overall, this is a focused experimental neuroscience study with clear relevance to respiratory control and airway defense. Its immediate footprint is modest, but the combination of open access publication, data-citation support, and a well-defined mechanistic question gives it a strong foundation for future citation and use as the literature matures.</p>",
